\title[Row-inclusion residual dynamics]{Row-Inclusion Residual Dynamics on Boolean Relations:\\Preorder Two-Cycles and Exact Fibres}
\author{Anonymous}
\date{}
\subjclass[2020]{37P05, 06A07, 05A15, 03G15}
\keywords{finite dynamics, Boolean relation, relational residual, preorder, Boolean lattice, induced order embedding, fibre formula}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For an $n\times n$ Boolean matrix $A$, replace $A$ by the matrix that records
inclusion among its row supports.  The first image is always a labelled
preorder, and on every preorder the update is transpose.  We derive the
global identity $\T_n^3=\T_n$: nonpreorders have tail one, equivalence
relations are exactly the fixed points, and all other preorders form strict
transpose two-cycles.  This gives every temporal fixed-set count and the
dynamical zeta function.  We also solve the one-step inverse problem for
every target.  For a preorder $P$, preimages are canonically bijective to
labelled induced order-embedding maps from its antisymmetric quotient into
the Boolean lattice $\B_n$.
Encoding Boolean coordinates as upper sets yields an explicit
inclusion--exclusion formula for $|\T_n^{-1}(P)|$; nonpreorder targets have
empty fibre.  Relational self-residuation and its preorder property are
standard background and receive no contribution credit.  Exact exhaustion
through $n=4$ is used only for falsification, and external release remains
on hold.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle
\enlargethispage{2pt}

\section{The literal map and theorem}

Let $\B_n=2^{[n]}$ be the Boolean lattice.  For a Boolean matrix
$A\in\{0,1\}^{n\times n}$, write
\[
 R_i(A)=\{j\in[n]:A_{ij}=1\}.
\]
Define the literal self-map
\begin{equation}\label{eq:map}
 \T_n(A)_{ij}=1
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 R_i(A)\subseteq R_j(A).
\end{equation}
We represent a preorder $P$ on $[n]$ by the Boolean matrix with
$P_{ij}=1$ exactly when $i\le_Pj$.  Let $q_n$ denote the number of labelled
preorders on $[n]$, and let $B_n$ denote the $n$th Bell number.

For a finite preorder $S$, let
\[
 J(S)=|\Up(S)|
\]
be its number of upper sets.  Given a preorder $P$, define
$i\sim_Pj$ when $i\le_Pj$ and $j\le_Pi$, and let $Q=P/{\sim_P}$ be the
resulting quotient poset.  Put
\begin{equation}\label{eq:DQ}
 D_Q=\{(q,r)\in Q^2:q\not\le_Qr\}.
\end{equation}
For $S\subseteq D_Q$, let $Q_S$ be the preorder obtained by adjoining every
pair in $S$ to $Q$ and taking reflexive--transitive closure.

\begin{theorem}[Complete forward and inverse dynamics]\label{thm:main}
For every $n\geq1$, the following hold.
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\roman*)}]
\item The image of $\T_n$ is precisely the set of labelled preorders, and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:transpose}
                       \T_n(P)=P^{\mathsf T}
\end{equation}
for every preorder $P$.  In particular,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:T3}
                             \T_n^3=\T_n.
\end{equation}
\item Every nonpreorder has tail exactly one.  The fixed points are exactly
the equivalence relations; every other preorder belongs to a strict
transpose two-cycle.  Consequently, for every $k\geq1$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fixed-iterates}
 |\Fix(\T_n^k)|=
 \begin{cases}
 B_n,&k\text{ odd},\\
 q_n,&k\text{ even},
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
and the Artin--Mazur zeta function is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:zeta}
 \zeta_{\T_n}(z)
 =(1-z)^{-B_n}(1-z^2)^{-(q_n-B_n)/2}.
\end{equation}
\item A target outside the preorders has empty fibre.  For every preorder
$P$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fibre}
 |\T_n^{-1}(P)|
 =\sum_{S\subseteq D_Q}(-1)^{|S|}J(Q_S)^n.
\end{equation}
Equivalently, the fibre is canonically bijective to the set of labelled
induced order-embedding maps $Q\hookrightarrow\B_n$; no quotient by
automorphisms of $Q$ is taken.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

The operation in \eqref{eq:map} is a row-oriented instance of relational
self-residuation.  Schmidt's \cite[\S4.4, especially Fig.~4.4.2,
p.~45]{Schmidt2011} right-residual convention relates a row of the numerator
to the rows of the denominator that it contains; reversing that displayed
orientation gives exactly the row-containment convention in \eqref{eq:map}.
Relation algebra and containment preorders are standard.  Principal-upper-set
representations are classical \cite[pp.~525--526]{Botts1954}, and induced
embeddings in Boolean lattices belong to an established order-theoretic
literature \cite{KatonaNagy2015}.  Our scope is the iterated finite map and
its complete inverse atlas, not invention of any of those constructions.

\section{Collapse to a preorder and order reversal}

\begin{lemma}[The residual preorder]\label{lem:preorder}
For every Boolean matrix $A$, the relation $\T_n(A)$ is reflexive and
transitive.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Every row support contains itself, so $R_i(A)\subseteq R_i(A)$ gives
reflexivity.  If $R_i(A)\subseteq R_j(A)$ and
$R_j(A)\subseteq R_k(A)$, then $R_i(A)\subseteq R_k(A)$, giving
transitivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Principal upper sets reverse inclusion]\label{lem:reverse}
If $P$ is a preorder, then $\T_n(P)=P^{\mathsf T}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Row $i$ of $P$ is the principal upper set
$\mathord{\uparrow}i=\{x:i\le_Px\}$.  We claim
\[
 \mathord{\uparrow}i\subseteq\mathord{\uparrow}j
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad j\le_Pi.
\]
If the inclusion holds, reflexivity puts $i$ in
$\mathord{\uparrow}i$, hence in $\mathord{\uparrow}j$, so $j\le_Pi$.
Conversely, $j\le_Pi\le_Px$ implies $j\le_Px$ by transitivity.  Substitution
in \eqref{eq:map} proves the lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{thm:main}(i)--(ii)]
By \cref{lem:preorder}, the image is contained in the preorders.  Conversely,
if $P$ is a preorder then so is $P^{\mathsf T}$, and
\cref{lem:reverse} gives
\[
                         \T_n(P^{\mathsf T})=P.
\]
Thus every preorder occurs in the image.  Applying
\cref{lem:reverse} twice after the first update gives \eqref{eq:T3}.

A state is periodic only if it lies in the image, hence only if it is a
preorder.  On the preorder image the map is transpose, so every period is one
or two.  A preorder is fixed exactly when it is symmetric.  A reflexive,
transitive, symmetric relation is an equivalence relation, and the converse
is immediate.  Equivalence relations on $[n]$ are counted by $B_n$; the
remaining $q_n-B_n$ preorder states pair into strict cycles.

Equation \eqref{eq:fixed-iterates} follows directly.  Finally, using
$\zeta_{\T_n}(z)=\exp(\sum_{k\geq1}|\Fix(\T_n^k)|z^k/k)$, or multiplying one
factor for each cycle, yields \eqref{eq:zeta}.
\end{proof}

The numbers $q_n$ also count finite labelled topologies, a classical
correspondence and enumeration problem \cite{ErneStege1991}.  We use this
census only to state the periodic population; it is not part of the residual
claim.

\section{Quotient-poset geometry of a fibre}

\begin{proposition}[Fibre as an induced embedding set]\label{prop:embedding}
Let $P$ be a preorder and $Q=P/{\sim_P}$.  Sending a source matrix $A$ to
its row-support map induces a bijection to labelled embedding maps
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bijection}
 \T_n^{-1}(P)
 \longleftrightarrow
 \{f:Q\hookrightarrow\B_n:
       q\le_Qr\Longleftrightarrow f(q)\subseteq f(r)\}.
\end{equation}
No automorphism quotient is taken: the elements of $Q$ are the distinguished
equivalence classes of the fixed labelled target $P$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $\T_n(A)=P$.  By definition,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:iff}
 i\le_Pj\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad R_i(A)\subseteq R_j(A).
\end{equation}
If $i\sim_Pj$, both inclusions in \eqref{eq:iff} hold and the rows are
equal.  Conversely, equal rows force both relations, so the row assignment
descends to an injective map $f:Q\to\B_n$.  The two directions in
\eqref{eq:iff} say exactly that $f$ preserves and reflects order.

Conversely, from an induced order embedding $f$ assign row support $f([i])$
to every $i$.  Order preservation and reflection then make
\eqref{eq:iff} hold, so the resulting Boolean matrix maps to $P$.  The two
constructions are inverse.
\end{proof}

The quotient is essential.  Mutual comparability in a preorder is exactly
the equality forced among source rows; treating all $n$ labels as distinct
would incorrectly demand an injective embedding before this forced collapse.

\section{Every-target inclusion--exclusion}

\begin{lemma}[Coordinate encoding]\label{lem:coordinates}
For any finite preorder $S$, the number of order-preserving maps
$f:S\to\B_n$ is $J(S)^n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For coordinate $c\in[n]$, put
\[
 U_c=\{q\in S:c\in f(q)\}.
\]
The map $f$ is order preserving exactly when every $U_c$ is an upper set.
The $n$ coordinates are independently chosen, giving $J(S)^n$.  Conversely,
the chosen upper sets recover $f(q)=\{c:q\in U_c\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{thm:main}(iii)]
A nonpreorder cannot be a target by \cref{lem:preorder}.  Fix a preorder
$P$ and its quotient poset $Q$.  Begin with all order-preserving maps
$f:Q\to\B_n$.  For each $(q,r)\in D_Q$, let $E_{q,r}$ be the bad event
\[
                             f(q)\subseteq f(r).
\]
Avoiding every such event is precisely order reflection, and hence, by
\cref{prop:embedding}, membership in the desired fibre.

If all events indexed by $S\subseteq D_Q$ are imposed, then $f$ must preserve
the original order of $Q$ together with every adjoined comparison in $S$.
This is equivalent to being order preserving for the reflexive--transitive
closure $Q_S$.  By \cref{lem:coordinates}, the number of such maps is
$J(Q_S)^n$.  Inclusion--exclusion over the bad events gives \eqref{eq:fibre}.
\end{proof}

The formula remains valid when added comparisons identify elements in
$Q_S$: upper sets depend only on the resulting preorder.  Standard
inclusion--exclusion and poset enumeration are used in their ordinary senses
\cite{Stanley2012}; no algorithmic novelty is asserted.

\section{Exact audit and limitations}

The paper-local verifier exhausts all $2^{n^2}$ matrices for $n\leq4$.  For
each source it independently checks preorder formation,
$\T(P)=P^{\mathsf T}$, and $\T^3=\T$.  It then compares the formula
\eqref{eq:fibre} with direct fibre counts for every preorder target.
\Cref{tab:audit} is a compact fingerprint of the frozen run.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Exact theorem-interface audit.  ``Strict states'' counts states in
strict two-cycles, not cycles.}
\label{tab:audit}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrrr}
\toprule
$n$ & states & preorders & fixed & strict states & min fibre & max fibre\\
\midrule
1 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 2\\
2 & 16 & 4 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 5\\
3 & 512 & 29 & 5 & 24 & 8 & 24\\
4 & 65,536 & 355 & 15 & 340 & 16 & 600\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The run contains 265,050 exact assertions.  Enumeration supplies no step of
the proofs and says nothing about novelty beyond the tested boxes.

A second, independently implemented lane does not call the
inclusion--exclusion evaluator.  It enumerates labelled maps into Boolean
lattices, tests order preservation and reflection, expands each map
class-by-class to a source matrix, and compares the resulting set with every
direct fibre through $n=4$.  It also checks all 219 labelled four-element
posets in $\B_5$.  That lane executes 13,238,845 further exact assertions;
it too is only counterexample pressure.

There are also substantive limitations.  Formula \eqref{eq:fibre} is exact
but exponential in the number of missing ordered pairs; we do not claim an
efficient general evaluator.  It does not simplify the still-difficult
global enumeration of labelled preorders.  Most importantly, the first-step
self-residual construction is owned relation-algebra background.  A bounded
source search did not locate the precise iterated-plus-inverse conjunction,
but a non-hit is not a priority or novelty certificate.

\section{Conclusion}

Row-support inclusion sends the full Boolean-relation cube onto the preorder
stratum in one step, and principal upper sets turn the next update into order
reversal.  This produces a complete one-step-tail, one/two-cycle dynamical
atlas.  Quotienting mutual comparability exposes the inverse geometry:
source rows are labelled induced Boolean-lattice representation maps, and upper-set
coordinates make every fibre accessible to inclusion--exclusion.  These
statements form an internally verified theorem package; external release,
priority, authorship, and submission remain on hold.

